<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ title }}</title>
<meta name="description" content="Analysis of the Northwind sample database: market performance, restocking priorities and logistics efficiency, with reproducible Python code.">
<style>
:root {
  color-scheme: light;
  --font-sans: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
  --page: #f9f9f7; --card: #fcfcfb; --ink: #0b0b0b; --ink-2: #52514e; --muted: #6f6e69;
  --line: #e1e0d9; --accent: #2a78d6; --accent-soft: #edf4fd; --action: #0f7a55; --action-soft: #eef8f3;
  --note-bg: #f0efec; --link: #1c5cab;
  --chart-surface: #fcfcfb; --grid: #e1e0d9; --axis: #c3c2b7; --band: #efeee9;
  --series-1: #2a78d6; --series-2: #eb6834; --series-3: #1baf7a; --negative: #e34948;
  --neutral-mark: #a9a8a1;
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme="light"]) {
    color-scheme: dark;
    --page: #0d0d0d; --card: #1a1a19; --ink: #f0efec; --ink-2: #c3c2b7; --muted: #a3a29b;
    --line: #2c2c2a; --accent: #3987e5; --accent-soft: #15243a; --action: #4cc495; --action-soft: #132a21;
    --note-bg: #242422; --link: #86b6ef;
    --chart-surface: #1a1a19; --grid: #2c2c2a; --axis: #4a4a46; --band: #2a2a28;
    --series-1: #3987e5; --series-2: #d95926; --series-3: #199e70; --negative: #e66767;
    --neutral-mark: #c3c2b7;
  }
}
:root[data-theme="dark"] {
  color-scheme: dark;
  --page: #0d0d0d; --card: #1a1a19; --ink: #f0efec; --ink-2: #c3c2b7; --muted: #a3a29b;
  --line: #2c2c2a; --accent: #3987e5; --accent-soft: #15243a; --action: #4cc495; --action-soft: #132a21;
  --note-bg: #242422; --link: #86b6ef;
  --chart-surface: #1a1a19; --grid: #2c2c2a; --axis: #4a4a46; --band: #2a2a28;
  --series-1: #3987e5; --series-2: #d95926; --series-3: #199e70; --negative: #e66767;
  --neutral-mark: #c3c2b7;
}
* { box-sizing: border-box; }
body { margin: 0; background: var(--page); color: var(--ink); font: 17px/1.6 var(--font-sans); }
main { max-width: 880px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 56px 16px 96px; }
a { color: var(--link); }
p { margin: 0 0 16px; }

/* Hierarchy */
.eyebrow { text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 8px; }
h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.6rem); line-height: 1.15; margin: 0 0 16px; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
h2 { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3.4vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.25; margin: 0 0 16px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 0 0 8px; }
.lede { font-size: 1.12rem; color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 62ch; }
.meta { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }
header { margin-bottom: 48px; }

/* At a glance */
.glance { display: grid; gap: 16px; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr)); margin: 0 0 72px; }
.glance article { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 14px; padding: 24px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
.glance h3 { font-size: 0.8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 12px; }
.glance p { font-size: 0.98rem; }
.glance ul { margin: 0 0 16px; padding-left: 18px; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
.glance a { margin-top: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; }

/* Question sections */
section.question { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 16px; padding: 40px; margin: 0 0 56px; }
.why { color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 64ch; margin-bottom: 28px; }
.callout { border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px 24px; margin: 0 0 32px; }
.callout .label { display: block; font-size: 0.75rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; }
.finding { background: var(--accent-soft); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); }
.finding .label { color: var(--accent); }
.finding p { font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0; font-weight: 500; }
.meaning { margin: 8px 0 28px; }
.meaning .label { display: block; font-size: 0.75rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; font-weight: 700; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 6px; }
.meaning p { margin: 0; }
.action { background: var(--action-soft); border-left: 4px solid var(--action); }
.action .label { color: var(--action); }
.action ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; }
.action li { margin: 4px 0; font-weight: 500; }

/* Metric cards, priorities, badges */
.glance article .metric { font-size: 2.6rem; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.02em; margin: 0 0 4px; }
.glance article .metric-label { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 16px; }
.glance article .card-action { font-size: 0.93rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding-top: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px; }
.badge { display: inline-block; font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
         border-radius: 999px; padding: 2px 8px; margin-right: 6px; vertical-align: 1px; white-space: nowrap; }
.badge.confirmed { background: var(--action-soft); color: var(--action); border: 1px solid var(--action); }
.badge.tentative { background: var(--note-bg); color: var(--ink-2); border: 1px dashed var(--muted); }
.badge.to-verify { background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent); border: 1px solid var(--accent); }
section.priorities { margin: 0 0 72px; }
.priorities ol { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 16px 0; display: grid; gap: 16px; }
.priorities li { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 14px; padding: 20px 24px;
                 display: grid; grid-template-columns: 56px 1fr; gap: 4px 16px; }
.priorities .rank { grid-row: span 3; font-size: 2.2rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent); line-height: 1; }
.priorities .owner { font-size: 0.75rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--muted); font-weight: 700; }
.priorities h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; }
.priorities p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
.legend-note { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); }
.guide { background: var(--note-bg); border-radius: 12px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: -16px 0 32px; font-size: 0.92rem; }
.guide p { margin: 0 0 12px; }
.guide-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 8px; }
.guide-grid div { background: var(--card); border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px 10px; font-size: 0.85rem; }
.guide-grid strong { display: block; }
.guide-axis { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 8px; font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--muted);
              text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; margin-bottom: 6px; text-align: center; }
.buy { margin: -16px 0 32px; }
.buy h3 { font-size: 1rem; margin: 0 0 4px; }
.buy .how { font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 70ch; }
dl.glossary { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap: 8px 20px; font-size: 0.93rem; }
dl.glossary dt { font-weight: 600; }
dl.glossary dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-2); }
@media (max-width: 600px) { dl.glossary { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } dl.glossary dd { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .guide-grid, .guide-axis { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .guide-axis { display: none; } }

/* Charts */
figure { margin: 8px 0 32px; }
.chart-title { font-size: 1rem; margin: 0 0 12px; }
.chart { overflow-x: auto; }
.chart svg { display: block; width: 100%; min-width: 560px; height: auto; }
figcaption { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; margin-top: 8px; max-width: 70ch; }

/* Expandable details */
details { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 14px 0; }
details:last-of-type { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
details > summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--ink-2); list-style-position: outside; }
details > summary:hover { color: var(--ink); }
details[open] > summary { margin-bottom: 16px; }
details .inner { font-size: 0.93rem; }
details ul { padding-left: 20px; }
.note { background: var(--note-bg); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--ink-2); }

/* Tables */
.table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 12px 0 20px; }
table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; min-width: 720px; font-size: 0.84rem; }
caption { text-align: left; caption-side: top; padding-bottom: 6px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }
th, td { padding: 7px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
th { color: var(--ink-2); font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }
td:last-child { min-width: 240px; }
td.num, th.num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap; }

/* Closing sections */
section.plain { margin: 72px 0 0; }
section.plain ul { padding-left: 20px; color: var(--ink-2); }
code { font-size: 0.88em; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
footer { margin-top: 72px; padding-top: 24px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }

@media (max-width: 600px) {
  body { font-size: 16px; }
  main { padding-top: 32px; }
  section.question { padding: 24px 18px; }
  .callout { padding: 16px 18px; }
}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>

{% macro data_table(t, caption) %}
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <caption>{{ caption }}</caption>
  <thead><tr>{% for h, n in t.headers %}<th scope="col"{% if n %} class="num"{% endif %}>{{ h }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for row in t.rows %}<tr>{% for v, n in row %}<td{% if n %} class="num"{% endif %}>{{ v }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>
  {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
{% endmacro %}

{% macro badge(status) %}<span class="badge {{ status|lower|replace(' ', '-') }}">{{ status }}</span>{% endmacro %}

{% macro question(num, q, owner, heading, why, chart_title, caption, support_title, support_caption, table_caption, method) %}
<section class="question" id="q{{ num }}" aria-labelledby="q{{ num }}-h">
  <p class="eyebrow">Question {{ num }} · {{ owner }}</p>
  <h2 id="q{{ num }}-h">{{ heading }}</h2>
  <p class="why"><strong>Why it matters.</strong> {{ why }}</p>

  <div class="callout finding">
    <span class="label">Key finding</span>
    <p>{{ q.key }}</p>
  </div>

  <figure>
    <h3 class="chart-title">{{ chart_title }}</h3>
    <div class="chart">{{ q.main_chart | safe }}</div>
    <figcaption>{{ caption }}</figcaption>
  </figure>
  {% if caller %}{{ caller() }}{% endif %}

  <div class="meaning">
    <span class="label">Business interpretation</span>
    <p>{{ q.meaning }}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout action">
    <span class="label">Recommended action</span>
    <ul>{% for a in q.actions %}<li>{{ badge(a.status) }}{{ a.text }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
  </div>

  <details>
    <summary>Supporting chart and detailed table</summary>
    <div class="inner">
      <figure>
        <h3 class="chart-title">{{ support_title }}</h3>
        <div class="chart">{{ q.support_chart | safe }}</div>
        <figcaption>{{ support_caption }}</figcaption>
      </figure>
      {{ data_table(q.table, table_caption) }}
    </div>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Full interpretation</summary>
    <div class="inner">
      <p>{{ q.answer | safe }}</p>
      <ul>{% for item in q.interpretation %}<li>{{ item }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>How this was analysed</summary>
    <div class="inner">
      <p>{{ method }}</p>
      <p class="note">{{ q.caveat }}</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>
{% endmacro %}

<header>
  <p class="eyebrow">Northwind sample database · orders to {{ cutoff }}</p>
  <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
  <p class="lede">Three questions a manager can act on, answered from 830 orders placed between July 1996 and May 1998 by a small food importer and wholesaler.</p>
  <p class="meta">{% if repo_url %}<a href="{{ repo_url }}">Code and data on GitHub</a> · {% endif %}Built {{ built_at }}</p>
</header>

<section class="glance" aria-label="At a glance">
{% for item in summary %}
  <article>
    <h3>{{ item.label }}</h3>
    <p class="metric">{{ item.metric }}</p>
    <p class="metric-label">{{ item.metric_label }}</p>
    <p>{{ item.finding }}</p>
    <p class="card-action">{{ badge(item.action.status) }}{{ item.action.text }}</p>
    <a href="#{{ item.anchor }}">See the evidence →</a>
  </article>
{% endfor %}
</section>

<section class="priorities" aria-labelledby="prio-h">
  <h2 id="prio-h">Top three management priorities</h2>
  <p class="why">Ranked across departments by how certain the evidence is, how much money is at stake each month, and how quickly the step can be taken.</p>
  <ol>
  {% for p in priorities %}
    <li>
      <span class="rank">{{ p.rank }}</span>
      <span class="owner">{{ p.owner }} · {{ badge(p.status) }}</span>
      <h3>{{ p.title }}</h3>
      <p>{{ p.why }} <strong>Next step:</strong> {{ p.next }}</p>
    </li>
  {% endfor %}
  </ol>
  <p class="legend-note">{{ badge("Confirmed") }}holds in every check we ran. {{ badge("Tentative") }}depends on which period is compared or isn't statistically clear: test it before committing resources. {{ badge("To verify") }}a lead to check before acting.</p>
</section>

{% call question(1, q1, "Sales leadership",
   "Which markets grew or declined, which accounts drove it, and where should sales attention go?",
   "Sales attention is limited. This shows where extra effort is likely to pay off, and where revenue hangs on a single customer.",
   "Where each market stands: share change against dependence on one customer",
   "Each bubble is a market, sized by revenue. The lines divide the chart into six suggested focuses; the shaded band marks markets near the 50% concentration line.",
   "Which accounts drove the change in the four largest markets",
   "The accounts whose revenue changed most in each focus market, in dollars.",
   "Market scorecard",
   "Revenue is compared between the same two quarters a year apart (Q4 1996 + Q1 1997 against Q4 1997 + Q1 1998). Each market is measured on the change in its share of company revenue, so a market growing exactly as fast as the company scores 0%, and on its largest customer's share of the market. Outperforming means +15% or more relative to the company; underperforming means −10% or less; high concentration means one customer holds over 50%.") %}
  <div class="guide">
    <p><strong>How to read this chart.</strong> <em>Across:</em> how the market's share of company revenue changed. Right of the +15% line it grew faster than the company; left of the −10% line it fell behind. <em>Up:</em> how much of the market's revenue comes from its single largest customer; above the 50% line, one customer holds most of it.</p>
    <div class="guide-axis"><span>Losing ground</span><span>Keeping pace</span><span>Gaining ground</span></div>
    <div class="guide-grid">
      <div><strong>Rescue</strong>Losing ground and reliant on one customer: find out which account is the cause.</div>
      <div><strong>Protect</strong>Keeping pace but reliant on one customer: look after that account.</div>
      <div><strong>Broaden</strong>Growing, but on one customer: keep it and grow others.</div>
      <div><strong>Diagnose</strong>Losing ground with a broad customer base: call the accounts that fell.</div>
      <div><strong>Maintain</strong>Keeping pace with a broad base: no change needed.</div>
      <div><strong>Invest</strong>Growing with a broad base: the best case for more sales effort.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endcall %}

{% call question(2, q2, "Purchasing",
   "Which active products should purchasing restock first?",
   "Stock-outs lose sales and over-ordering ties up cash. This tells purchasing what to order first.",
   "How urgent each product is: demand against months of stock left",
   "Each mark is an active product: lower means fewer months of stock, further right means higher demand. Numbers mark the top " ~ shortlist_size ~ " priorities.",
   "How far the top " ~ shortlist_size ~ " are from one month of stock",
   "Units available (in stock plus on order) against one month of recent demand; the gap is the least an order would need.",
   "Top " ~ shortlist_size ~ " restocking priorities (full ranking in the appendix)",
   "Recent demand is units ordered in the last 90 days, as a monthly rate. Months of stock is stock divided by that rate. Critical: out of stock, or under half a month even counting stock on order. High: under a month on hand, with too little on order. Watch: below the reorder level, or thin cover without a reorder rule or with erratic demand. Within each tier, products are ranked by the monthly revenue at stake.") %}
  <div class="buy">
    <h3>What to order first</h3>
    <p class="how"><strong>How these products were chosen:</strong> every active product is first placed in an urgency tier by how many months of recent demand its stock covers (Critical, then High, Watch, OK). Within each tier, products are ranked by monthly revenue at stake (units sold per month × average price). These are the first {{ buy_list_size }}. The order range covers one month of demand at the long-run rate (low end) and at the recent peak rate (high end).</p>
    {{ data_table(q2.buy_list, "Top " ~ buy_list_size ~ " products to order") }}
  </div>
{% endcall %}

{{ question(3, q3, "Operations",
   "How do processing times and freight costs vary across shipments, and where are the biggest opportunities to improve logistics?",
   "Late dispatches cost customer goodwill and freight is a cost on every order. This shows whether to fix Northwind's own process or renegotiate with carriers.",
   "Late dispatches by how much time the customer allowed",
   "Share of shipped orders sent after the customer's required date.",
   "Where dispatch time is lost",
   "Order-days that would be saved if each group's orders were dispatched like the best comparable group's. Groups overlap (every order has a carrier, an employee and a destination), so these are never added together.",
   "Largest opportunities, including freight gaps that do not survive adjustment",
   "Processing time is days from order to dispatch for the " ~ q3.overview.ShippedOrders ~ " shipped orders; the " ~ q3.backlog.Orders ~ " unshipped orders are reported separately. Groups are compared only with orders of similar size, and each group's gap is measured against the best comparable group. A regression checks whether carrier differences survive once order size, destination and employee are taken into account.") }}

<section class="plain" aria-labelledby="lim-h">
  <h2 id="lim-h">What this analysis can't tell us</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Profit:</strong> there are no product costs, so everything is about revenue.</li>
    <li><strong>Cause:</strong> the comparisons show where things differ, not why.</li>
    <li><strong>Delivery:</strong> dates record dispatch, not delivery.</li>
    <li><strong>Timing of stock-outs:</strong> the stock snapshot is undated and there are no supplier lead times.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="meta">Data that would close these gaps: product costs, carrier delivery dates, dated stock records, supplier lead times, sales territories and freight terms.</p>
</section>

<section class="plain" aria-labelledby="gloss-h">
  <h2 id="gloss-h">Terms in plain English</h2>
  <dl class="glossary">
    <dt>Share of company revenue</dt><dd>A market's slice of all of Northwind's sales.</dd>
    <dt>Relative to the company</dt><dd>Whether a market grew faster or slower than Northwind as a whole. 0% means it kept exactly the same slice.</dd>
    <dt>Concentration</dt><dd>How much of a market's sales come from its single largest customer. Above 50%, losing that one customer would cost most of the market.</dd>
    <dt>Months of stock</dt><dd>How long the stock on the shelf would last at the recent rate of sales.</dd>
    <dt>Critical / High / Watch / OK</dt><dd>Urgency tiers. Critical: out of stock, or under two weeks even counting stock on order. High: under a month, with too little on order. Watch: below its reorder level or close to it. OK: nothing urgent.</dd>
    <dt>Revenue at stake</dt><dd>A product's sales per month at recent demand: what's at risk if it runs out.</dd>
    <dt>Possible shortage</dt><dd>More units are waiting on unshipped orders than are in stock. Worth checking; not a confirmed backorder.</dd>
    <dt>Processing time</dt><dd>Days from the customer's order to the day Northwind sends it out.</dd>
    <dt>Late dispatch</dt><dd>An order sent out after the date the customer asked for.</dd>
    <dt>Slow order</dt><dd>One of the slowest 10% of orders (more than 18 days from order to dispatch).</dd>
    <dt>Order-days</dt><dd>Extra days of waiting added up across orders: 10 orders each 2 days slower is 20 order-days.</dd>
    <dt>Likely range</dt><dd>The range the true figure probably falls in, given how few orders there are (a 95% interval).</dd>
    <dt>Adjusted comparison</dt><dd>A comparison that holds other things equal, such as order size and destination, so that like is compared with like (a regression).</dd>
    <dt>Resampling check</dt><dd>Re-running the numbers many times on random reshuffles of the orders to see how much the result could move by chance (a bootstrap).</dd>
    <dt>Confirmed / Tentative / To verify</dt><dd>Confirmed holds in every check; tentative depends on the period compared or isn't statistically clear; to verify is a lead to check before acting.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="plain" aria-labelledby="app-h">
  <h2 id="app-h">Technical appendix</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>About the data and caveats</summary>
    <div class="inner"><ul>
      <li>1996 and 1998 are part-years, so growth compares the same quarters a year apart.</li>
      <li>Most countries place about one order a month, so smaller countries are grouped into regions.</li>
      <li>Only 5 customers placed their first order after mid-1997: changes in revenue come from existing accounts.</li>
      <li>The stock snapshot has no date and there are no supplier lead times: the restocking list is a priority order, not a forecast.</li>
      <li>Dates record when Northwind dispatched an order, not when it was delivered.</li>
      <li>There are no product costs, so everything is about revenue, not profit.</li>
    </ul></div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Q1: quarterly shares, uncertainty and robustness</summary>
    <div class="inner">
      <figure><h3 class="chart-title">Each market's share of company revenue by quarter</h3>
        <div class="chart">{{ appendix.q1_quarterly_chart | safe }}</div>
        <figcaption>Full quarters only.</figcaption></figure>
      {{ data_table(appendix.q1_uncertainty, "Bootstrap 90% intervals for relative growth (2,000 resamples of all company orders)") }}
      {{ data_table(appendix.q1_robustness, "Performance level under alternative definitions") }}
      {{ data_table(appendix.q1_windows, "Change in share of company revenue under three period comparisons (company growth: " ~ appendix.q1_window_growth.values()|join(", ") ~ ")") }}
      {{ data_table(appendix.q1_movers, "Largest account gains and losses in every market") }}
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Q2: full ranking, reorder rules and robustness</summary>
    <div class="inner">
      {{ data_table(appendix.q2_watch, "All active products, ranked") }}
      {{ data_table(appendix.q2_no_rule, "Active products with no reorder level, with suggested levels") }}
      {{ data_table(appendix.q2_discontinued, "Discontinued products still holding stock") }}
      {{ data_table(appendix.q2_robustness, "Critical/High products under other demand windows") }}
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Q3: breakdowns, regression and robustness</summary>
    <div class="inner">
      <figure><h3 class="chart-title">Days from order to dispatch, by carrier</h3>
        <div class="chart">{{ appendix.q3_carriers_chart | safe }}</div>
        <figcaption>Boxes show the middle half of orders; dots are individual slow orders.</figcaption></figure>
      {% for name, t in appendix.q3_breakdowns.items() %}{{ data_table(t, "Processing and freight by " ~ name|lower) }}{% endfor %}
      {% if appendix.q3_regression %}
      <p>Regression run because {{ appendix.q3_regression_reason }}. Freight: log(freight) on carrier, log(units), log(revenue) and destination. Dispatch: days on carrier, size band, destination, employee and quarter. Robust (HC1) standard errors.</p>
      {{ data_table(appendix.q3_regression, "Carrier effects compared with the best carrier") }}
      {% endif %}
      {% if appendix.q3_regression_without_slow %}
      <p>To check whether the dispatch gaps above come only from the slowest orders, the same regression is repeated with the slowest 10% of orders removed.</p>
      {{ data_table(appendix.q3_regression_without_slow, "Carrier effects with the slowest 10% of orders removed") }}
      {% endif %}
      {{ data_table(appendix.q3_robustness, "Do the top 3 opportunities stay in the top 5?") }}
      <p>Small-order consolidation: {{ appendix.q3_consolidation.Orders }} small orders have freight above 15% of revenue ({{ "%.1f"|format(appendix.q3_consolidation.ShareOfAllFreight * 100) }}% of all freight), so it isn't a material opportunity.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Verification</summary>
    <div class="inner">
      <p>Every headline number is recalculated a second way: the workbook is read cell by cell with openpyxl and the figures are computed in plain SQL (SQLite), sharing no code with the pandas pipeline. <strong>{{ appendix.checks_total[0] }} of {{ appendix.checks_total[1] }} checks match.</strong></p>
      <ul>{% for name, (ok, total) in appendix.checks_by_section.items() %}<li>{{ name }}: {{ ok }} of {{ total }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
      <p>Sample orders, products and totals were also worked out by hand from the raw cells; all match (<code>verification/hand_checks.md</code>).</p>
      {% if appendix.review %}
      <h3>Independent review</h3>
      <p>A reviewer that hadn't seen the conclusions recomputed the headline claims from the raw workbook and tried to break them. Each objection and how it was resolved:</p>
      <ul>{% for r in appendix.review %}<li><strong>{{ r.claim }}</strong> ({{ r.severity }}): {{ r.objection }} <em>Resolution:</em> {{ r.resolution }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Methods and definitions</summary>
    <div class="inner"><ul>
      <li><strong>Revenue</strong> is price charged × quantity × (1 − discount), counted by order date.</li>
      <li><strong>Relative growth</strong> is the change in a market's share of company revenue: 0% means it grew exactly as fast as the company.</li>
      <li><strong>Concentration</strong> is the largest customer's share of the market over the latest four quarters; above 50% is high.</li>
      <li><strong>Recent demand</strong> is units ordered in the last 90 days (6 Feb–6 May 1998), as a monthly rate.</li>
      <li><strong>Months of stock</strong> is stock ÷ recent monthly demand.</li>
      <li><strong>Processing time</strong> is days from order to dispatch. Slow orders are the slowest 10%.</li>
      <li><strong>Opportunity</strong> is excess days (or freight dollars) per order compared with the best comparable group in the same order-size band, times the group's orders.</li>
    </ul></div>
  </details>
</section>

